## 3. Prioritized Recommendations

### Tier 1 — Fix Before Next Live Session

| # | Fix | Effort | File |
|---|-----|--------|------|
| 1 | ~~Stop order config (GTC + outsideRth + triggerMethod)~~ | ✅ Done | [ibkr_client.py](file:///c:/Users/bwang/Documents/GitHub/CL_Analyst/src/live_execution/ibkr_client.py) |
| 2 | **Fix continuous→front-month contract in bracket order** | ~5 min | [live_trader.py](file:///c:/Users/bwang/Documents/GitHub/CL_Analyst/src/live_execution/live_trader.py) L789 |
| 3 | **Add position state tracking** — after each bar, verify the bracket orders are still active. If orphaned (position open but no SL/TP), re-protect immediately. | ~2 hours | [live_trader.py](file:///c:/Users/bwang/Documents/GitHub/CL_Analyst/src/live_execution/live_trader.py) |

### Tier 2 — Execution Logic Improvements (Research + Implement)

| # | Improvement | Impact | Area |
|---|-------------|--------|------|
| 4 | **Time-based exit** — if the position has been held for 288 bars (24h, matching training horizon) without hitting TP or SL, close at market. The model has no predictive power beyond its horizon. | High | Trade execution |
| 5 | **Trailing stop** — once price moves +1×ATR in your favor, trail the stop to breakeven. This turns a potential loss into a zero. | High | Trade execution |
| 6 | **Backtest SL parameter sweep** — run the existing backtester with SL multipliers [0.75, 1.0, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0] and compare net P&L. | Medium | Research |

### Tier 3 — Model & Pipeline Improvements (Longer-Term Research)

| # | Improvement | Rationale |
|---|-------------|-----------|
| 7 | **Exit model** — train a separate model to predict *when to close an open position* (rather than relying purely on fixed bracket levels). This is the single biggest gap between your system and professional quant firms. | The buy model predicts entries. Nobody predicts exits. Today's scenario (stopped out before rally) is a textbook case for an intelligent exit model. |
| 8 | **Sell-signal model integration** — your EXP-020 short model exists but isn't used in live. A sell signal while holding long could serve as an early exit trigger. | Immediate value from existing work. |
| 9 | **Regime filter** — add a macro-level regime detector (trending vs ranging vs high-vol) and only trade in regimes where the model has historically performed well. | Many losing trades cluster in specific regimes. |
| 10 | **Re-entry logic** — after a stop-out, if the model probability exceeds a *higher* threshold (e.g., 0.55 instead of 0.45), allow re-entry. | Prevents most revenge trades while allowing high-conviction re-entries. |

### Where to Focus

**The biggest bang for your buck is in trade execution logic (Tiers 1–2), not the model itself.** Your model has 87% precision and 84% win rate in backtesting — that's excellent. The gap is between *what the model predicts* and *how the live system acts on it*. Specifically:

1. **The model was trained assuming exact bracket execution** → the live system needs to enforce that contract (time exits, position monitoring, order health checks)
2. **The model has no exit intelligence** → the bracket is a crude proxy. An exit model or trailing stop would capture much of the value the fixed bracket leaves on the table
3. **The model ignores context after entry** → professional systems continuously re-evaluate the position. If post-entry features turn strongly bearish, they exit early rather than waiting for the stop

The model and feature engineering are already strong. The live execution pipeline is where the production gap is widest.
